Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.085
Film Courage: What is dilemma?

00:00:01.085 --> 00:00:05.700
Jen Grisanti, Speaker/Story/Career Consultant,&nbsp;
Author: Dilemma is something that I can't believe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:16.020
I didn't know the value of dilemma  when I&nbsp;
was an executive. I cannot believe in all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:25.140
scripts I analyzed over 12 and a half years that I&nbsp;
didn't have it termed. I think I loosely understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:37.740
dilemma in terms of story and conflict however I&nbsp;
it wasn't until after I left the studio and dived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:48.360
into story in every way imaginable that the word&nbsp;
dilemma came into my realm. I understood dilemma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:57.720
through a structuralist Jeff Kitchen yes that&nbsp;
that I first took one of my very first structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:07.560
courses from when I became an executive and when&nbsp;
I left the studio I re-looked at what I learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:18.960
from Jeffrey kitchen and dilemma somehow just came&nbsp;
to me in this way that changed how I see story and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:28.020
that became everything for developing my brand&nbsp;
and looking at the value of dilemma I feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:38.760
so many story people skip over or don't define&nbsp;
dilemma and dilemma is everything to setting up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:46.260
a story when we feel how the character is caught&nbsp;
between a rock and a hard place and we for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:53.700
I think the aha moment that went beyond what&nbsp;
I learned from Jeff was the recognition that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.700 --> 00:02:02.820
the choice the character makes in the dilemma is&nbsp;
what defines the external pursuit so that was my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:12.300
big aha moment that helped me I think there's so&nbsp;
much that we learn from everybody who's studying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:22.020
story I've been analyzing story for 30 years now&nbsp;
and when I look at my counterparts who think in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:29.160
alignment like Jeffrey Kitchen like Michael&nbsp;
Hague like John York they're they're when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:39.840
have people wear Dara Marks that are seeing story&nbsp;
in a similar way and you kind of look at like what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:47.520
is their terminology versus my terminology and&nbsp;
what what is aligning and what is missing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:56.280
how do you redefine something so that the writer&nbsp;
can grasp it more is dilemma the same as problem

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:07.080
dilemma I feel like is something that everyone&nbsp;
in life understands because we all face dilemmas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:19.440
continuously so I think the difference between a&nbsp;
dilemma and a problem a problem is more of like an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:31.620
obstacle versus a dilemma is creating a pursuit&nbsp;
so a problem or obstacle is more something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:42.000
happens to a character on the way to a goal versus&nbsp;
a dilemma is a trigger event that was another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:49.620
thing that I added to dilemma was the recognition&nbsp;
that a trigger event happens to the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:56.640
before they face the dilemma so that was something&nbsp;
that was missing and what I understood of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:03.120
before as well and that was something that I was&nbsp;
like ah that's what was missing so when there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:12.540
trigger event that leads a character to a dilemma&nbsp;
then the the choice that they make in that dilemma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:21.000
defines a pursuit so that's what differentiates&nbsp;
a dilemma from a problem and some of that is is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:28.560
set up before the actual the story starts because&nbsp;
it's a childhood wound yes yes I have come to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:39.300
in the way I teach and see story that the stories&nbsp;
that are hitting at the highest level are stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:47.160
where it's almost like a math equation it's&nbsp;
it's almost like you're looking at the trigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:55.500
event that happens in childhood then you're often&nbsp;
looking at a trigger event that happens before we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:06.660
enter the story and then the inciting incident&nbsp;
or series trigger and dilemma in TV will split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:16.920
open those two wounds and then you're developing&nbsp;
the character from those three places as a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:23.640
add depth to the character how does a character's&nbsp;
backstory influence their goals and their dilemmas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:34.860
when you look at in life and we think about like&nbsp;
I think a TV show that I feel like perfected the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:43.260
idea I think when loss first started and it looked&nbsp;
at memories in our life it looked at because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:50.400
way that loss developed characters at first&nbsp;
was the idea of going back to a specific moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:58.800
in a person's life and kind of arcing that out&nbsp;
and then when I think about the way it evolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:09.360
was a recognition that the flashback sequence&nbsp;
was recognizing that it isn't just one moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:18.780
in the arc of that moment that influences our&nbsp;
choices in the present it is a series of moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:31.380
from different life traumas or Joys or failures&nbsp;
that inform choice so by going to different life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:39.900
places then then we feel what is influencing&nbsp;
why the character is choosing the way they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:50.520
how do past wounds hold a character back I like&nbsp;
to think that when you look at the past wound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.720 --> 00:07:00.060
you're looking at starting with the childhood&nbsp;
wound you're looking at something called a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:06.420
negative narrative that we can all identify&nbsp;
with the voices we hear in our head that tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:13.560
us I'm not enough I'm not good enough I'm not&nbsp;
worthy of this that starts with the childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:23.040
wound and then progresses from that negative&nbsp;
narrative in the mind that goes into a flaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:32.580
and when you go into the flaw the flaw is what's&nbsp;
holding a character back and again we all identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:40.920
with flawed Behavior we all have flaws and we&nbsp;
all become conscious of our flaws does not mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:49.200
we heal our flaws we become conscious and story&nbsp;
is the opportunity for the character to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:59.520
conscious of that flaw and decide if they want&nbsp;
to acknowledge the flaw or if they want to resist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:08.220
working or processing through that flaw in order&nbsp;
to get to the goal have you seen it where writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:22.020
make their characters too perfect or too flawed&nbsp;
both yes both I feel the the the characters that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:30.300
are too perfect are characters that we can't&nbsp;
identify with by characters it used to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:36.900
that when you read scripts and you would read&nbsp;
a character description you would read oh she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:46.980
beautiful or he's handsome and it started sounding&nbsp;
like rubber like you you can't tell anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:55.320
about a character from that description and I&nbsp;
think writers have gotten to a place where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:03.600
recognize the value of describing a personality&nbsp;
trait is going to bring you much deeper into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:10.920
that character and understanding that character&nbsp;
how many dilemmas should a main character face

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:18.120
in this story there are two pivotal places for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:27.960
dilemma the two pivotal places are in the&nbsp;
inciting incident setup or in the climax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:36.960
the value of having a dilemma in both places&nbsp;
is a tremendous value because it shows you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:46.560
is the character going to transform or not when&nbsp;
you look at the the structure that I've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:57.480
shows and films do where you have the character&nbsp;
often facing a similar dilemma in the climax that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:06.000
they faced in the inciting incident then the&nbsp;
question becomes did they learn through going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:15.780
through action obstacles and stakes did they&nbsp;
learn what the flaw was that was holding them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:25.320
back and through that recognition are they able&nbsp;
to move forward so if the writer puts a dilemma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:33.360
